    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a pretty strong stance. You're saying that any state move toward surveillance is essentially \"authoritarian creep.\" \n\nIsn't that a bit of a stretch? If a government uses targeted, lawful surveillance to prevent a specific terrorist attack or child exploitation, aren't they actually upholding their end of the bargain by protecting the community? Why is that kind of targeted measure considered a fundamental violation rather than a legitimate tool of governance?",
